Oakbridge Knowledge Base
Detailed Product Capabilities & Operating Flows
Knowledge Admin + The Oakbridge Way  |  Client Discussion Document  |  August 2026
This document describes how the Oakbridge Knowledge Base works in production terms: how content is authored, governed, published, consumed inside The Oakbridge Way, controlled by access rules, extended with documents and GitHub foundations, and prepared for AI assistants — always with human approval before knowledge becomes organizational truth.
Two product surfaces work together:
•	Knowledge Admin — authoring, review, publishing, administration, documents, and GitHub foundations.
•	The Oakbridge Way — reading and searching approved articles, and linking knowledge to goals, items, and day-to-day work.
Publishing rule: Approve = Publish. Only content in Approved (Published) status is shown to readers in The Oakbridge Way and used as the source of truth for search and assistants.
1. Content Authoring
Authors write knowledge in a Markdown editor designed for operational documentation, not just plain notes. The editor supports three working modes so writers can move fluidly between drafting and quality checking:
•	Edit — full Markdown drafting surface.
•	Preview — rendered reading view of the same content.
•	Split — side-by-side edit and preview for faster iteration.
Supported content structures include tables, images, lists, checklists, fenced code, Mermaid diagrams, callout blocks, mathematical equations, and media links (including video). This allows policies, playbooks, implementation notes, and process guidance to live in one governed format.
New content begins as Draft. Authors cannot create an article already in Approved status. That constraint is intentional: publishing is always an explicit governance action.
2. Content Governance — Draft, Review, Approval
Knowledge Admin uses a clear editorial lifecycle:
Status	What it means	Who typically acts
Draft	Work in progress; editable by authorized authors; not visible to general readers.	Author / Editor
In Review	Submitted for quality and accuracy review; waiting for approval decision.	Approver / Admin / Owner
Approved (Published)	Live organizational knowledge; available in The Oakbridge Way and approved search/assistant paths.	Approver / Admin / Owner
2.1 Publishing lifecycle (end-to-end)
1.	Author opens a workflow/article under the Knowledge Admin hierarchy and creates or edits Markdown content.
2.	Content is saved as Draft.
3.	Author selects Submit for Review → status becomes In Review.
4.	Reviewer opens the article or the Content Review queue.
5.	Reviewer either Approve Content (publish) or Send Back to Draft for rework.
6.	On approval, a version snapshot is recorded, search/index refresh is attempted, and The Oakbridge Way cache is invalidated so readers see current published knowledge.
 
Figure 1 — Article Publishing Lifecycle (Draft → In Review → Approved)
If an Approved article is later edited as live content by someone with live-edit rights, the working body returns to Draft until it is re-approved. Prior published history remains available in version snapshots.
3. Article History — Versions & Restore
Each approval creates an immutable version snapshot. This gives the organization an audit trail of what was published and when, and protects against accidental loss of prior wording.
From Version History, authorized users can compare versions and choose Restore as Draft. Restore never publishes automatically. The restored wording becomes Draft working content; teams must submit and approve again before it becomes live.
 
Figure 2 — Version History and Restore-as-Draft
•	History rows are retained; restore appends a new historical record rather than erasing the past.
•	Readers continue to see the previously Approved published body until a new approval occurs.
•	This model supports rollback, auditability, and controlled republishing.
4. Content Suggestions — Safer Collaboration on Live Content
Not everyone who can contribute should silently overwrite published knowledge. When an article is Approved and a collaborator does not have live-edit rights, they use Suggest Changes instead of direct edit.
1.	Collaborator opens an Approved article and submits a proposed Markdown update (optionally with a reason).
2.	The suggestion is stored separately with status pending; live content remains unchanged.
3.	Owner / assigned reviewer / authorized approver reviews a diff of live vs proposed content.
4.	Decision options: Approve & Merge (apply and re-approve), Request Changes, or Reject.
 
Figure 3 — Content Suggestion Flow (live content protected until acceptance)
Client takeaway: Suggestions enable contribution at scale without risking uncontrolled replacement of published knowledge. Live content changes only after an explicit accept-and-approve decision.
5. Knowledge Navigation
Knowledge Admin organizes content in a structured hierarchy so teams can manage large knowledge estates:
Knowledge Base → Journey → Category → Bucket → Article/Workflow
Administrators and authors use this structure for placement, ownership context, browsing, and operational management.
In The Oakbridge Way, readers use a focused Knowledge experience optimized for consumption:
•	Knowledge Base catalog / home
•	Category browsing
•	Article reading
•	Search across approved articles
This separation is deliberate: administration needs depth; day-to-day users need clarity and speed.
6. Search and Reading in The Oakbridge Way
Signed-in users with Knowledge access can search and read Approved articles inside The Oakbridge Way.
1.	User authenticates to The Oakbridge Way.
2.	Access is evaluated (KB role and/or valid scoped context).
3.	User browses Knowledge or searches for approved articles.
4.	Article body is returned only when the article is Approved for reader consumption.
 
Figure 4 — How published knowledge reaches The Oakbridge Way
Draft and In Review content remain outside the reader surface. Unauthorized users receive a clear access-denied experience rather than partial unpublished content.
7. Contextual Links — Goals, Items, and Tickets
Published knowledge is most valuable when connected to active work.
7.1 Goals and Items (The Oakbridge Way)
•	From a Goal or Item, users with permission can attach related Approved Knowledge Base articles.
•	Relationship types support clear intent: Supports, References, Implementation, Policy.
•	Links are bidirectional for navigation context; unlinking removes the association without deleting the article.
7.2 Tickets (Knowledge Admin engineering context)
•	Articles can also be linked to engineering/ticket systems (for example Jira, ServiceNow, Azure DevOps) for operational traceability.
•	This complements Goal/Item links: strategy and execution on one side, engineering workflow on the other.
8. Access Management
Access is designed in layers so organizations can be open where appropriate and strict where required.
Layer	What it controls
Sign-in / platform role	Authenticated identity; elevated platform roles (for example Super Admin) where applicable.
KB role	Baseline product capability: Reader, Editor, Admin.
Feature permissions	Fine-grained administration of view/create/edit/publish, submit/approve/reject review, user management, GitHub, and related admin features.
Scoped grants	Limits access to specific Knowledge Bases or hierarchy nodes when broad access is not desired.
Content ownership	On Approved articles, live edit vs Suggest Changes depends on ownership and permission — protecting published truth.
 
Figure 5 — Access Control Layers
In practice, the combination of role, feature permission, scoped grant, and ownership determines whether a person can read published knowledge, author drafts, submit for review, approve publishing, or administer integrations.
9. Documents and GitHub Foundations
9.1 Managed documents
Knowledge Admin supports managed document uploads (such as PDF/DOCX) with versioned handling and hierarchy linking. After upload, documents move through processing into a ready state for administrative use and indexing. This allows supporting material to live alongside canonical articles without replacing the article approval model.
1.	Authorized admin uploads a document scoped to a hierarchy node / Knowledge Base.
2.	System validates and stores the file, then processes/extracts content.
3.	A document version is recorded; indexing is attempted for retrieval use.
4.	Document remains administratively managed and searchable as reference material.
9.2 GitHub synchronization foundations
GitHub integration provides a foundation for aligning engineering documentation with the Knowledge Base:
•	Connect repositories and configure branches/paths and destination Knowledge Base mapping.
•	Sync on demand or via repository events.
•	Ingested material can be staged according to configured publish mode (draft / review / publish).
•	Default posture for generated/imported knowledge remains human-controlled: draft or review before live approval unless an organization explicitly configures otherwise.
Client takeaway: Treat GitHub sync as an operational foundation for bringing documentation into governance — not as an unsupervised replacement for human publishing judgment.
10. AI Readiness — Retrieval with Human Approval
Search and retrieval tooling is available for assistants and integrations. The design principle is consistent with reading:
•	Assistants and retrieval paths are built to use Approved knowledge as the authoritative source.
•	Draft and In Review content are not treated as organizational truth for reader/assistant answers.
•	Generated or imported material is intended to land under review and receive human approval before becoming live approved content.
1.	Knowledge is authored and approved in Knowledge Admin.
2.	On approval, version authority is recorded and retrieval indexes are refreshed where applicable.
3.	Assistant/search asks a question and retrieves from approved sources (and related managed evidence where configured).
4.	Answers can be grounded with citations/sources for transparency.
5.	Misses can inform knowledge-gap follow-up so humans close content holes deliberately.
11. How It All Fits Together
Stage	Where it happens	Result
Author & structure	Knowledge Admin	Draft knowledge in hierarchy with rich Markdown
Govern & publish	Knowledge Admin review workflow	Approved (Published) + version snapshot
Collaborate safely	Suggestions on Approved articles	Proposed changes without overwriting live content
Consume	The Oakbridge Way	Browse/search/read approved knowledge
Connect to work	Goals, Items, Tickets	Contextual links at the point of need
Control access	Roles, features, scoped grants, ownership	Least-privilege administration and publishing
Extend sources	Managed documents & GitHub foundations	Broader governed evidence under admin control
AI assist	Retrieval / assistant tooling	Approved-source answers with human publish gate
In one sentence: Knowledge Admin creates and governs the truth; The Oakbridge Way puts approved truth into daily work; access and approval rules keep unpublished material from becoming organizational fact too early.
